DISCLOSURE — npm Dual-Use Content Policy declaration

avanti is a general-purpose developer tool: a CLI that assembles local files
from a declarative YAML specification written by the user.

Capabilities that may resemble dual-use behavior to automated scanning:

1. Remote file retrieval: avanti downloads file content from sources the user
   explicitly lists in their local configuration file — plain HTTPS URLs,
   GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket repositories, git remotes, AWS S3 buckets, and
   HashiCorp Vault secrets.

2. Shell command execution: the user's configuration may define `exec:` sources
   and `post:` processors, which run user-specified shell commands locally and
   use their output as (or to transform) file content.

3. Local file writes: fetched and processed content is written to local file
   paths chosen by the user in the same configuration.

Intended legitimate use: keeping shared configuration files (linter configs,
CI workflows, editor settings, policy files, etc.) in sync across many
repositories and machines from declarative, version-controlled specs.

All network access, command execution, and file writes are driven solely by
the explicit, local, user-authored configuration file. avanti contains no
embedded remote endpoints, no telemetry, no auto-update mechanism, and no
functionality that runs without the user invoking the CLI against their own
configuration. Every write is previewed as a diff and requires interactive
confirmation unless the user passes an explicit flag.

avanti is open source (MIT): https://github.com/udondan/avanti
Every release is published with provenance attestations from GitHub Actions.
